  "method": "composition-based complementarity; no folding, no docking, no affinity prediction",
  "disclaimer": "WHAT THIS IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT -------------------------------- These sequences were designed by COMPOSITION-BASED COMPLEMENTARITY: the pocket profile says which chemistry a binder should present, and this script picked amino acids that present it, in roughly the right proportions, at a sensible length. That is ALL it did. In particular it did NOT: * fold the peptide or model its structure, * dock it into the pocket or place any residue at any coordinate, * compute, estimate or imply a binding affinity, Kd, IC50 or percentage chance of working, * check protease stability, aggregation, solubility in your buffer, or whether the peptide is even capable of reaching the site in a folded protein. Composition is a real signal - binders to greasy, negatively charged pockets really are enriched in greasy, positively charged residues - but it is a weak one on its own. Real structure-based mini-protein binder design uses RFdiffusion, ProteinMPNN, Rosetta and AlphaFold2 / AF-Multimer, which build an actual 3D interface and score it. If this project ever claims otherwise, the claim is wrong. Treat the list as a cheap first panel to express and screen, and expect most of it to do nothing. That is the normal outcome for a composition-only method, not a failure of your bench work."
